{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices across most horizons (often by ~2–7 €/t). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underestimation (lag): when the recent 30d history is falling, the model follows the direction but not the magnitude; realized declines are steeper and earlier. Increase negative drift / accelerate trend continuation in the first 1–20 days.",
    "Overshoot after local rebounds: after short-term upticks in history (e.g., last few days rising), the model projects too strong/too sustained a rebound; realized prices revert lower. Damp rebound amplitude and shorten rebound duration (stronger mean reversion).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: the overprediction tends to widen with horizon (mid-to-long horizon especially). Use a horizon-increasing downward correction (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Volatility/turning-point miss: model produces smoother paths and misses sharp sell-offs; incorporate asymmetric shock risk (bigger downside tails) during bearish regimes, rather than symmetric smoothing."
  ]
}
